# Virtual environment -- must never be committed. Streamlit Cloud builds its
# own environment from requirements.txt. None currently exists in this repo,
# but excluded defensively in case one gets created locally later.
venv/
.venv/
env/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Secrets -- TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN/CHAT_ID and (optionally) KALSHI_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD
# are read from env vars / .env locally, or Streamlit Cloud's Secrets panel in
# production (see .streamlit/secrets.toml.example). Never commit the real values.
.env
.streamlit/secrets.toml

# Kalshi API private keys. The RSA private key signs TRADING requests -- anyone
# holding it can place orders on the account. Kalshi shows it exactly once at
# generation and cannot re-issue it, so a leaked key means revoke + regenerate.
# Never commit one, in any environment, demo included.
*.pem
*.key
kalshi_private_key*.txt

# Logs -- regenerable, and paper_trader.log / bot.log grow unbounded.
*.log

# --- Data: read this before changing ---
# model/strike_prob_model.pkl and its metadata ARE committed on purpose --
# unlike a shelved/unused experiment, this is the ACTIVE fitted model
# (model/strike_probability.py) that dashboard/app.py calls directly for the
# live Scoreboard "Model read" card, and that paper_trader.py uses for real
# entry decisions. It's ~4KB (a 5-coefficient logistic regression). Excluding
# it would silently break the deployed dashboard.
#
# data/validation/*.json ARE committed on purpose too -- tiny, self-contained
# summaries the Model performance page reads directly.
#
# Personal trading record -- keep OUT of the repo, same as the sports models'
# bet_log.csv. paper_trader.py runs locally only (Windows Scheduled Task); a
# committed snapshot would just go stale the moment Cloud can't write back to
# it, and it's the user's actual (paper, so far) trading history either way.
data/trade_log.csv

# bot.py's alert dedup state (which windows/target-hits it has already sent a
# Telegram message for). Local runtime state, same reasoning as trade_log.csv:
# it only has meaning on the machine actually running bot.py, and a stale
# committed copy would be actively harmful -- a fresh clone would start up
# believing it had already alerted on windows it never saw and silently
# suppress them. Added 2026-08-04 with the persisted-state fix.
data/bot_alert_state.json

# live_executor.py's REAL order log. Same reasoning as trade_log.csv -- a
# personal trading record, meaningless off the machine that produced it, and
# not something to publish. Kept deliberately separate from trade_log.csv so a
# real order can never be confused with a simulated one.
data/live_orders.csv

# live_trader.py runtime state: the daily loss-cap tally, and the kill-switch
# flag. Both are machine-local operational state, not source.
data/live_session.json
STOP_TRADING

# Bulk historical research data (Coinbase 1-min bars, Kalshi settled markets
# and candlesticks, feature tables, walk-forward predictions) -- large
# (~33MB total), fully regenerable via the scripts in each research/*/scripts/
# folder (see each folder's own README for the exact commands), and not
# needed by the deployed dashboard at all. The scripts and READMEs themselves
# ARE committed -- they're the actual documentation of how these numbers
# were produced.
research/*/data/
research/*/results/

# Local Claude Code dev-machine settings (permission allowlist etc.) --
# machine-specific, not shared config. .claude/launch.json (the dashboard
# run config, no secrets) is committed on purpose.
.claude/settings.local.json

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Singleton lock files (runtime state, machine-local)
.*.lock

# live_trader stdout/stderr redirect targets (runtime logs)
live_run.log
live_run.err

# diagnostic + backup artefacts (contain live account state)
data/balance_diagnostic.json
data/*.bak
live_supervisor.log
